"""Original, finite multi-question reading passages for offline evidence practice.

All questions here were written to a particular passage. They are not official
College Board or ACT materials or full-length ACT passages. The central design
constraint: each answer must be entailed by an identifiable part of the text.
"""

PASSAGES = [
("""In 1998, a coastal research station began collecting water-temperature measurements every hour. At first, the archive seemed to show a steady warming trend. When a second team examined the instruments, however, it found that the station had moved its sensor twice: first from a shaded pier to an exposed dock, and later into deeper water. The second move occurred during a cool year, while the first occurred during a warm one. Rather than subtracting a single fixed correction from the whole record, the researchers compared each period with readings from three nearby stations that had not moved. The corrected record still showed warming, but the year-to-year spikes were less dramatic. The team published both versions alongside the dates of the sensor moves.""",[
 ('What is the most defensible conclusion about the original record?',
  'It likely exaggerated some short-term variations but did not invent the overall warming pattern.',
  ['All warming was caused by moving the instruments.','The corrected record showed no change over time.','The nearby stations were necessarily wrong.'],
  'After station comparisons, the long-term trend remained, but the spikes became less pronounced.','Distinguish the existence of a trend from how large individual spikes appear.','Evidence interpretation'),
 ('Why did the researchers use nearby, unmoved stations?',
  'To help separate sensor-location effects from broader changes in water temperature.',
  ['To prove all nearby waters were identical.','To replace every original observation with a guess.','To show temperature could not be measured hourly.'],
  'Stable reference stations provide a comparison for shifts introduced by instrument placement.','What variable differs between the station and its comparison sites?','Experimental design'),
 ('What is the function of the final sentence?',
  'It shows that the researchers preserved information needed to evaluate their adjustments.',
  ['It implies their correction was mathematically perfect.','It discredits their entire investigation.','It says the sensor was never moved.'],
  'Publishing both series and move dates makes the analysis more transparent without proving it infallible.','What can a reader independently inspect because of that disclosure?','Reading inference'),
]),
("""A nonprofit tested a text-message reminder designed to increase attendance at free evening classes. Students at one learning center who opted into reminders attended more sessions than students who did not. The nonprofit initially attributed the difference to its messaging. A program evaluator noticed that students could opt in only during an optional orientation session; those who attended orientation had already demonstrated a willingness to spend extra time on the program. The evaluator proposed a new trial in which students would be randomly assigned to receive or not receive reminders after registering. Staff objected that some students might consider the absence of reminders unfair. The evaluator suggested telling all participants the purpose of the study and offering everyone access to reminders after the trial ended.""",[
 ('Which factor most directly threatens the initial causal conclusion?',
  'The students receiving reminders had self-selected through an optional orientation.',
  ['Attendance was recorded in sessions rather than minutes.','The classes were held in the evening.','The program did not charge tuition.'],
  'Orientation attendance is related to motivation and also determined eligibility for reminders, creating selection bias.','What pre-existing difference could explain both receiving a text and attending class?','Causal inference'),
 ('How would random assignment improve the proposed study?',
  'It would make motivational differences less likely to systematically determine who receives reminders.',
  ['It would force every student to attend every class.','It would guarantee an increase in attendance.','It would eliminate the need to record attendance.'],
  'Random assignment reduces systematic confounding; it does not guarantee any particular effect.','What does randomization balance on average rather than promise to change?','Experimental design'),
 ('What concern does the evaluator’s final proposal attempt to address?',
  'The ethical cost of withholding a potentially useful service from some participants.',
  ['The cost of sending a large number of text messages.','The scientific need to compare two different schools.','The possibility that the orientation would be cancelled.'],
  'Informed participation and later access respond to the fairness objection.','Connect the staff’s objection to the proposed remedy.','Reading inference'),
]),
("""At the turn of the twentieth century, a city council argued that expanding its port would attract manufacturing businesses. A shipping company’s annual reports, which the council frequently cited, projected major gains in cargo volume. Yet a historian reviewing property records found factories purchasing land inland before construction on the new docks began. Those factories were connected by rail to an older port in another city. A second historian cautioned that buying land is not the same as opening a factory: some firms purchased property years in advance. Both historians agreed that the new docks eventually handled substantial cargo. Their disagreement concerned which economic changes the project began and which it merely accelerated.""",[
 ('What does the property-record evidence most directly challenge?',
  'The claim that the dock expansion initiated all local industrial interest.',
  ['The fact that new docks eventually handled cargo.','The possibility that railroads existed before 1900.','The accuracy of every shipping-company report.'],
  'Land purchases before construction show some investment interest predated the docks, though not necessarily operating factories.','Which event happened first? Which claim does that timing weaken?','Cross-text connections'),
 ('How does the second historian qualify the first historian’s point?',
  'By noting that an early land purchase need not mean a factory had already begun operating.',
  ['By claiming the properties had never been bought.','By proving factories were built solely because of the docks.','By stating the new docks carried no cargo.'],
  'The distinction between purchase and operation limits what can be inferred from property transactions.','Find the difference between a plan and a completed action.','Evidence interpretation'),
 ('Which statement would both historians most likely accept?',
  'The port eventually became economically important, even if its role in initiating growth remains disputed.',
  ['The council’s forecasts were guaranteed to be accurate.','All inland factories opened before the port expansion.','Economic activity began only after the new docks opened.'],
  'The final sentences explicitly identify agreement on later cargo activity and disagreement on timing.','Look for the shared claim rather than either side’s strongest claim.','Reading inference'),
]),
("""The poet Mara Venn revised a collection for nearly a decade. Early drafts used elaborate descriptions of landscapes; the published poems are sparer, sometimes naming only a single object in each stanza. A critic interpreted this compression as evidence that Venn had grown indifferent to the natural world. In a notebook written during the revision, however, Venn described removing adjectives so that a reader might notice the specific shapes of stones and grasses without being told what to feel. Another critic argued that the shorter descriptions therefore represented not a retreat from observation but an experiment in directing attention. The notebook was private and never intended as a public explanation, though it remains relevant evidence about the poet’s intentions.""",[
 ('What is the strongest challenge to the first critic’s interpretation?',
  'The poet’s notes link shorter description to a deliberate focus on observed details.',
  ['The published poems include more adjectives than the drafts.','A private notebook always determines a poem’s only valid meaning.','The poet stopped writing about landscapes entirely.'],
  'The notebook supports an intentional attentional strategy, which contrasts with indifference.','Which sentence directly connects revision choices to an artistic goal?','Cross-text connections'),
 ('Why does the passage specify that the notebook was private?',
  'To qualify its use as evidence of intention without treating it as a definitive public interpretation.',
  ['To argue the notebook contains no factual statements.','To show the poet wanted nobody to read her poems.','To prove the published collection was unfinished.'],
  'Private intent is relevant but cannot exhaust how the public work is interpreted.','How does the final concession limit the evidence’s authority?','Evidence interpretation'),
 ('As used in the passage, “compression” most nearly refers to',
  'achieving an effect with fewer descriptive words.',
  ['making the poems physically smaller on the page.','removing all references to nature.','writing with more elaborate ornament.'],
  'The published poems are described as sparer than the early drafts.','Look for the transformation from drafts to publication.','Words in context'),
]),
("""Archaeologists excavating a hill settlement discovered ceramic bowls whose mineral composition matched clay from a valley eighty kilometers away. One team suggested that the bowls were traded to the settlement after being fired in the valley. A second team noticed fragments of unfired clay with a similar composition in the settlement itself and proposed that potters had transported raw materials instead. Neither explanation, the teams agreed, could be established solely by matching mineral signatures. They planned to compare microscopic marks left by different firing techniques and to examine whether the bowls’ shapes resembled local or valley traditions. A report discussing the excavation concluded that the material evidence confirmed contact between the two regions but not yet the precise form of that contact.""",[
 ('What can be concluded from the mineral matches alone?',
  'Materials connected to the valley reached the settlement, but the form of exchange remains uncertain.',
  ['Every bowl was definitely fired in the valley.','All the clay must have come from the settlement itself.','There was no contact between the regions.'],
  'Both finished-bowl trade and raw-material transport explain the composition; contact is the shared implication.','What feature is compatible with both proposed routes?','Evidence interpretation'),
 ('What would a match between settlement bowls and local firing marks most strongly support?',
  'The possibility that at least some bowls were produced at the settlement.',
  ['The claim that no clay ever left the valley.','The certainty that every bowl was made by one potter.','The claim that the settlement had no trading relationships.'],
  'Local firing traces would help distinguish local production from import of finished vessels.','What aspect of production would firing marks reveal?','Reading inference'),
 ('How do the teams’ interpretations differ?',
  'They disagree about whether the exchanged objects were finished bowls or raw materials.',
  ['They disagree about whether the bowls contained clay.','One denies that mineral compositions can be tested.','One claims the hill settlement was eighty kilometers high.'],
  'The two hypotheses propose distinct forms of material movement.','Identify what traveled in each proposed scenario.','Cross-text connections'),
]),
("""A workplace introduced a default retirement-savings contribution for newly hired employees. Most employees kept the default rate, though they could change it at any time. Within a year, average contributions were higher than among earlier hires who had been asked to select a rate themselves. Executives interpreted the pattern as proof that employees now preferred saving more. An outside analyst noted that taking no action is easier than making an active choice and that people sometimes treat defaults as recommendations. The analyst also pointed out that the newest hires had different salaries and ages than the earlier group. She recommended comparing similar employees who were randomly presented with different initial settings before drawing conclusions about underlying preferences.""",[
 ('Which statement is best supported by the evidence?',
  'A default contribution rate was associated with higher saving, but it does not by itself establish a change in preferences.',
  ['The default made all employees financially secure.','Every employee actively chose the contribution rate.','New hires necessarily preferred less saving than earlier hires.'],
  'The default may shape behavior through inertia or perceived recommendation, while the groups differ in other ways.','Does a choice made by inaction reveal the same preference as an active choice?','Causal inference'),
 ('Which additional concern does the analyst raise besides the default’s influence on behavior?',
  'The comparison groups differ in characteristics that might affect contribution levels.',
  ['The firm had no way to change the default.','The earlier hires were forbidden to save money.','The contribution rate had no numeric value.'],
  'Salary and age differences confound the before/after comparison.','Which characteristics were not held constant across employee cohorts?','Evaluating statistical claims'),
 ('The word “inertia” would most likely describe employees who',
  'leave an initial setting unchanged because changing it requires effort.',
  ['choose new rates after extensive research.','never receive a default option.','reject all automated systems on principle.'],
  'The preceding discussion emphasizes the ease of taking no action.','Connect the word to the contrast between doing nothing and changing settings.','Words in context'),
]),
("""A wildlife reserve restored a stretch of marsh to create habitat for migrating birds. Within five years, bird sightings increased sharply. Volunteers took most of the counts, and the reserve launched a publicity campaign that attracted many new birdwatchers during the same period. Some staff celebrated the counts as proof that bird populations had increased. A visiting ecologist cautioned that increased observation effort can produce more sightings even if the number of birds is unchanged. She proposed standardized surveys at fixed locations and times, along with a comparison to nearby unrestored wetlands. The reserve’s director agreed that the restoration was promising but argued that public participation was also a meaningful benefit, independent of population growth.""",[
 ('What alternative explanation for rising sightings is explicitly raised?',
  'More observers may have recorded a greater share of birds that were already present.',
  ['The birds stopped migrating entirely.','The reserve prohibited scientific measurements.','The marsh became completely dry after restoration.'],
  'The publicity campaign increased observation effort, which can affect counts independently of population changes.','Distinguish an increase in animals from an increase in recorded animals.','Causal inference'),
 ('Why does the ecologist propose standardized surveys?',
  'To reduce differences in measurement effort and provide a comparison for the restoration’s effect.',
  ['To guarantee the marsh restoration succeeded.','To remove all volunteers from the reserve permanently.','To determine bird populations without collecting observations.'],
  'Fixed methods and unrestored comparison sites directly target observation bias and other shared changes.','What would be controlled by fixed survey times and locations?','Experimental design'),
 ('How does the director’s response modify the staff’s original claim?',
  'It recognizes a separate public-engagement benefit while withholding certainty about population change.',
  ['It states that bird populations certainly declined.','It denies that volunteers contributed observations.','It rejects all future scientific monitoring.'],
  'Public participation may be valuable even before changes in bird numbers are verified.','What different outcome does the director count as meaningful?','Cross-text connections'),
]),
("""A student newspaper argued that a public transit discount should be judged primarily by how many additional trips it generates. A transit economist disagreed with the use of ridership as the only measure. A discount may help workers reach jobs they already travel to, she explained, without producing many new trips; that lower cost can still create a large financial benefit for families. She also warned that some additional trips would have occurred without the discount, so total ridership cannot be interpreted as the policy’s causal effect. She recommended evaluating affordability, access to employment, and the net cost of the program alongside changes in travel behavior.""",[
 ('What is the economist’s main criticism of a ridership-only evaluation?',
  'It overlooks benefits to existing riders and may mistake total trips for newly caused trips.',
  ['It assumes transit fares never change.','It claims discounted fares cannot reduce costs for riders.','It requires all travelers to switch to cars.'],
  'Existing riders can gain even without adding trips, and total trips do not measure trips caused by the policy.','Find the two distinct limitations mentioned by the economist.','Evidence interpretation'),
 ('Which new observation would most directly inform the affordability question?',
  'How much eligible workers spend on commuting before and after receiving the discount.',
  ['The paint color used on station walls.','The names of buses purchased twenty years ago.','The number of route maps printed last year.'],
  'Commuting expenditure measures the direct financial burden the discount aims to lower.','Which metric corresponds to the economic burden on families?','Evaluating statistical claims'),
 ('How does the economist’s argument relate to the newspaper’s?',
  'It broadens the criteria rather than claiming that ridership has no relevance.',
  ['It agrees that the number of trips is the only useful measure.','It declares all discounts harmful.','It rejects the possibility of measuring any outcome.'],
  'She suggests other criteria alongside ridership and distinguishes counts from causal impacts.','Is the response a total rejection or an extension of the criteria?','Cross-text connections'),
]),
("""In a short story, the narrator waits at an empty train station with a suitcase containing letters she has never sent. She repeatedly checks the departure board, even though the station clock has stopped. When a distant train finally appears, the narrator says that its sound is “almost an answer.” Yet she makes no move toward the platform. A literary scholar reads this hesitation as evidence that the narrator is less interested in catching a train than in delaying a decision about the letters. Another scholar agrees that the suitcase is important but cautions against assuming a single reason for the hesitation: the stopped clock and the uncertain arrival may also emphasize the narrator’s difficulty trusting her own sense of time.""",[
 ('Which detail most directly supports the first scholar’s reading?',
  'The narrator stays still even after a train becomes visible.',
  ['The train arrives at a different station.','The narrator has already sent every letter.','The clock resumes working precisely when she departs.'],
  'Remaining stationary despite an approaching train supports an interpretation of intentional delay.','Find the observed action that suggests a choice not to board.','Reading inference'),
 ('How does the second scholar qualify the first?',
  'By suggesting uncertainty about time as an additional explanation for the narrator’s hesitation.',
  ['By denying the suitcase appears in the story.','By proving the train is imaginary.','By claiming the narrator is eager to leave immediately.'],
  'The second scholar leaves the suitcase interpretation open but notes a separate motif.','What interpretation is added rather than negated?','Cross-text connections'),
 ('What does the phrase “almost an answer” suggest in context?',
  'The approaching train seems to offer a resolution that remains incomplete.',
  ['The train explicitly speaks to the narrator.','The letters have already received replies.','The station board provides a definite destination.'],
  'Almost indicates an incomplete or uncertain response, consistent with her continued hesitation.','How does the qualifying word change “an answer”?','Words in context'),
]),
("""A public-health survey reported that residents of one neighborhood walked more often than residents elsewhere in the city. The investigators had recruited participants through a popular walking club and used a smartphone app to track activity. A statistician warned that this method might overrepresent people who already enjoy walking and who own compatible phones. Rather than discarding the data, she recommended describing its findings as relevant to the recruited participants and collecting a new, more representative sample before making citywide claims. She also suggested comparing the app’s step counts with a small set of independently measured walks to check for measurement error.""",[
 ('Which limitation concerns the representativeness of the sample?',
  'Recruitment through a walking club may overrepresent people who already walk frequently.',
  ['Independent step measurements are always impossible.','The study tracked activity rather than food.','Participants lived in a neighborhood.'],
  'Recruitment linked to the behavior being studied creates selection bias.','Who was most likely to join the study?','Evaluating statistical claims'),
 ('What would the proposed independent measurements primarily evaluate?',
  'Whether the smartphone app’s recorded steps agree with observed steps.',
  ['Whether all smartphone owners prefer walking.','Whether the neighborhood has enough sidewalks.','Whether the walking club should close.'],
  'Cross-checking counts addresses measurement validity, separate from sampling bias.','Does the extra check change the sample or test how a variable is measured?','Experimental design'),
 ('What is the statistician’s main recommendation?',
  'Limit conclusions to the studied group until a representative sample supports broader claims.',
  ['Ignore all data from voluntary participants.','Treat the survey as proof that every resident walks often.','Replace step counts with guesses.'],
  'The statistician proposes appropriately scoped claims rather than rejecting the observations.','How does she preserve what the study legitimately shows?','Evidence interpretation'),
]),
]

EXTRA_READING=[]
for passage,items in PASSAGES:
    for question,answer,wrong,explanation,hint,skill in items:
        EXTRA_READING.append((passage,question,answer,wrong,explanation,hint,skill))
